applications already have it. This one does. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:04.960
the dream is coming true, we might just play&nbsp;
in a virtual copy of the real world very soon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:10.560
Especially that it is available in Unreal&nbsp;
Engine too through a paid plugin, though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:17.880
I am sure that free versions will pop up very&nbsp;
soon too. In fact, things are going so quickly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:24.320
I wouldn’t be surprised if by the time this&nbsp;
video appears, it is already available for free.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:31.800
And it gets even better. Way better! For instance,&nbsp;
we know that text to image is possible, but we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:40.560
talking about 3D virtual worlds here. So, oh my.&nbsp;
It has been just a few months of this paper being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:49.520
out, can that really be? Follow up papers are&nbsp;
already popping up. Wait a second. This is text to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:58.400
3D. We write something, and out comes 3D geometry.&nbsp;
Our last episode was on exactly that, and it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:06.320
already being surpassed. What is happening? Wow.&nbsp;
Well, what is happening is that Gaussian Splatting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:14.600
is already being used to improve these techniques.&nbsp;
Hmm, in many cases, I see more detailed geometry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:22.960
being produced. And all this in just a couple of&nbsp;
months and one more paper down the line. So cool!

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:28.320
But it doesn’t end there. Not even&nbsp;
close. Goodness! Here is another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:33.960
paper which is able to perform&nbsp;
image to 3D. One photo goes in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:41.840
and out comes 3D geometry. I already see that it&nbsp;
converges to a solution much quicker than earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:48.120
neural radiance fields-based solutions,&nbsp;
that is fantastic news, but come on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:56.440
what can it do? Wow, it really does it. In&nbsp;
